<h1>Abstract</h1>

Visual impairment affects millions of people worldwide, making everyday tasks such as reading text, identifying objects, recognizing currency, and recognizing familiar faces
significantly more difficult, which in turn reduces independence and raises safety concerns (WHO, 2026). Existing assistive applications such as Microsoft's Seeing AI and 
Be My Eyes offer valuable support, but they rely heavily on continuous internet connectivity and cloud-based processing, offer limited support for regional languages such as 
Urdu, and do not provide a fully private, locally managed face-recognition system. This leaves a clear gap for users in low-connectivity regions or those who are not fluent 
in English.
This project proposes Binai (بینائی), an offline-capable Android application that uses on-device artificial intelligence to assist visually impaired users without depending 
on cloud services. Binai integrates object detection, currency recognition, private face recognition, optical character recognition (OCR), voice commands, SOS alerts and 
reminders, and a Live Guidance feature, all supporting bilingual voice interaction in Urdu and English. The Live Guidance feature combines on-device object detection with 
Android's ARCore Depth API to detect nearby objects, estimate their distance, and deliver short spoken instructions in real time. By consolidating multiple assistive 
functions into a single, private, offline mobile application, Binai aims to reduce dependence on cloud infrastructure and fragmented tools, improve accessibility for 
  Urdu-speaking users, and offer a practical, independently achievable solution within a Final Year Project development cycle.
